From c6c26732acae32e9ef9a4187277294842fb5a26e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:25:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Offer the tab switcher as a list as well as a grid

Settings > Tabs and tab groups > Tab view chooses between the grid of cards the
switcher has always shown and a list of rows. Grid is what an absent preference
means, so every existing install keeps the switcher it already had.

A row is the favicon, the title and the domain, with the close button on the
end. That is the same data the grid card binds, read through the same shared
helpers, so a row and a card describe a tab the same way and the accessibility
text is the one the switcher already produced.

The list is a new TabListMode rather than a flag threaded through the grid, so
choosing Grid leaves every existing branch untouched.

Thumbnails are avoided rather than hidden. TabListCoordinator hands the mediator
no ThumbnailProvider in list mode, which the mediator already treats as "do not
fetch", so no fetcher is built, nothing is decoded and no bitmap is held for a
row. Chromium's own capture paths are left alone; they feed the Hub animation
and are a separate question.

A tab group stays one row, which is what the model already produces: the grid
collapses a group into a single card, so the list gets a single row with the
group's colour and resolved title, and tapping it opens the same group dialog.
That dialog is always a grid, as is the tab list editor, which has no selectable
row presentation and would otherwise be handed a list it cannot draw.

Message cards need nothing here. Every message service registers its own view
type on the coordinator through registerItemType, so they keep working in either
mode instead of arriving as an unknown type.

Five places assumed the tab list was a grid and had to be told otherwise. Each
one crashed the switcher on the device before it was found, so they are listed
rather than summarised:

- The layout manager is a GridLayoutManager one column wide, not a
  LinearLayoutManager. The pinned tab strip and the scroll position code both
  read the tab list's layout manager back as a GridLayoutManager.
- The pinned tab strip is not built for a list at all. It sizes itself from the
  grid card size, which a list never computes. Pinned tabs stay in the list as
  ordinary rows instead of in a strip above it.
- A list records its own item size, because the archived tabs message and
  anything else observing the tab list's item size assume there is one.
- The initial scroll offset centres a grid card in the viewport. A list has no
  card to centre, so the row is left at the top.
- The shrink and expand Hub animation grows a tab out of its card's thumbnail
  and reads the row back as a grid card. A list fades instead, which is what a
  low-end device already does.

Applying a change to Tab view needs the browser to start again. The mode is
read when a pane coordinator is built, and the switcher keeps its coordinator
across an ordinary visit, so the setting is deliberately read once rather than
swapped underneath a live switcher.

Migration note (156.0.8078.25): upstream deleted TabProperties.URL_DOMAIN and
getDomainForTab as unused (da10efe6c2c); the list rows read them, so they are
kept here, with group domains taken from getTabsInGroup(). The editor's
TabListMode.GRID argument is gone: upstream now hard-codes GRID in the editor
(d08ebccbb2e). pinned_tabs_strip is now pinned_tabs upstream.
